# zbc: pinned-source completion on top of 0001 + 0002.
#
# NOT a capture from a moving working copy. 0001 and 0002 are the verbatim
# local captures and are left byte-for-byte untouched; this patch is authored
# against the exact tree they produce, and says here what it changes and why.
#
# Base: upstream 419d153ca411d1698899992d86c51eb9a7e14919 + 0001 + 0002.
#       That base was re-derived from a pristine `git checkout` of the pinned
#       commit and reproduces source digest
#       bf5331d664e70890356797a1daaa2113efeb36fcb10d906760530c26ab0c07df,
#       the value manifest.tsv carried before this patch existed.
# Apply with: git -C <checkout> apply -p1 <this file>   AFTER 0001 and 0002.
# Authored: 2026-10-02T01:28:36Z (items 1-4), 2026-10-02T04:12Z (items 5-6).
# Resulting source digest (zbc source_digest in manifest.tsv):
#   b794e308392834b79892e36078c49cfd74598f67b26e069cd47a864013e5e3d2
#
# 1. COMPILE. Three defects made the pinned tree unbuildable under Zig 0.16.0.
#    src/lib.zig opened a `.{ … }` anonymous struct literal for writeFile's
#    options and closed it with `);`, so the parser reached the end of the test
#    still expecting `}`. src/type_engine/document_store.zig called the private
#    `requireAddressableSource` unqualified from DocumentStore, a different type
#    in the same file — Zig does not put one type's private members in another
#    type's scope; the call is now `Handle.requireAddressableSource`. In
#    src/flow/cfg_builder.zig the `LoweringError` set omitted
#    `error.SourceTooWide`, which `FileCache.init` already returns for a source
#    whose length is not representable as the `u32` token offsets `std.zig.Ast`
#    uses, so the `LoweringError!void` entry points could not `try` the
#    inferred `!void` helpers they delegate to.
#
# 2. RULE REGISTRATION. 0001 deleted the `double_optional_ptr` enum member,
#    shortened `all_invariants` from 113 to 112, and dropped the rule's catalog
#    entry, module import, dispatch row and `refAllDecls` pin, while leaving its
#    `.zig` and `.md` in the tree as unreferenced dead files. All of it is
#    restored from upstream 419d153 verbatim — the dispatch row uses 0001's
#    single-space table formatting like every neighbouring row — so `?*?T`
#    out-params are reported again.
#
# 3. SEVERITIES. 0001 downgraded seven rules from error to warning. Six go back
#    to upstream's `.@"error"`: memset_undef_after_len_truncation,
#    f32_narrowing_int_to_float, index_minus_one_without_zero_guard,
#    index_type_narrowing_wraparound, intcast_of_negated_signed and
#    midpoint_addition_overflow. The seventh, intfromfloat_without_clamp, STAYS
#    `.warning`: that is a pre-existing, explicitly accepted gate policy whose
#    three findings were each reviewed with a numeric-range proof in hand
#    (criticalRange, expRef) before this patch existed. It is now disclosed at
#    the call site and in tools/quality/PROVENANCE.md instead of passing for an
#    upstream default. No rule is disabled, no ZBC_STRICT is set, and no
#    interval arithmetic is altered to quiet anything.
#
# 4. SOURCE SELECTION. `SourceSelection.collect` had exactly one rejection path
#    that recorded nothing: an explicitly named input the policy refuses was
#    dropped with a bare `continue`, so `zbc vendor/thing.zig` analysed nothing,
#    printed nothing and exited 0 while the gate printed `zbc: OK`. All three
#    explicit-input rejections now record `error.SourceExcluded`, which prints
#    `zbc: cannot select <path>: SourceExcluded` and drives exit 2. The
#    discovery-driven exclusions in `walk()` are untouched and still silent:
#    there the operator named the parent directory, not the skipped entry, and
#    dropping dependency/vendor/build/out trees there is the policy working.
#
# 5. HEAP OWNERSHIP IN THE LIVENESS SOLVER. Before this, the tree compiled and
#    linked but the binary corrupted the heap on every file in its own src/.
#    `zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast` on a tree carrying items 1-4 reported
#    `run exe zbc failure / process terminated with signal SEGV`, and running the
#    binary over its own source killed all 152 files with SIGSEGV or SIGABRT and
#    stderr naming `double free or corruption (out)`, `realloc(): invalid
#    pointer` and `mremap_chunk(): invalid pointer`. A gdb backtrace pointed at
#    one place, with a null source and a non-zero length:
#      #4 array_list.ensureTotalCapacityPrecise (std/array_list.zig:1236)
#      #6 array_list.addOne   #7 array_list.append (item=1)
#      #8 flow.worklist.computeLiveness  (src/flow/worklist.zig:143)
#      #9 flow.worklist.Solver.init      (src/flow/worklist.zig:338)
#     #10 flow.worklist.check            (src/flow/worklist.zig:291)
#     #11 lib.analyzeEscape              (src/lib.zig:142)
#    computeLiveness does
#      var predecessors: std.ArrayListUnmanaged(std.ArrayListUnmanaged(BlockId)) = .empty;
#      try predecessors.resize(gpa, block_count);
#      … predecessors.items[successor].append(gpa, block.id);
#    and Zig 0.16's `ArrayListUnmanaged.resize` is only
#      try self.ensureTotalCapacity(gpa, new_len);
#      self.items.len = new_len;
#    (std/array_list.zig:1111-1114) — it grows the buffer and sets the length
#    and writes none of the new slots. Every element of `predecessors` was
#    therefore whatever the allocator returned; the first `append` grew that
#    garbage list and the deferred `deinit` freed it. It is a 0.17→0.16
#    semantics difference, and `predecessors` does not exist upstream at all.
#    The fix is the 0.16 idiom: `resize`, then initialise every element to
#    `.empty` before the first `append`.
#
#    VERIFIED: with this item in place the same `zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast`
#    gets past compile and the analyzer runs to completion —
#    `zbc: selected=153 analyzed=153 failed=0 findings=75` — with no SIGSEGV, no
#    SIGABRT and no allocator diagnostic on any file.
#
# 6. WHAT THAT BUILD THEN SHOWED, RECORDED AND NOT WORKED AROUND. The build's
#    remaining failure is `run exe zbc failure / process exited with error code
#    1`, and it is NOT a severity-policy consequence. build.zig:59-62 is
#    upstream's dogfood: `self_check.addDirectoryArg(b.path("src/"))` plus
#    `b.default_step.dependOn(&self_check.step)`, so every `zig build` runs
#    `zbc src/` on zbc itself, and main.zig:306 is
#      return if (all_problems.items.len != 0) 1 else 0;
#    — exit 1 on ANY finding, warning or error. This tree reports 75 findings on
#    its own source, so the self-check cannot pass for it at any severity
#    setting, including upstream's and including the one accepted advisory.
#    Nothing here suppresses, re-severitises, excludes or reinterprets a finding
#    to make that go away; the discrepancy between this captured source and
#    upstream's clean-dogfood expectation is recorded for whoever owns the next
#    decision. Restoring the six severities in item 3 does not create this: the
#    accepted `.warning` alone is already enough to fail the self-check.
#
#    The same audit should be applied to the one other uninitialised `resize` in
#    this tree: src/type_engine/analysis.zig:4166 resizes an `ArrayMap`'s entry
#    list and then writes only the key half of each entry. That is upstream code
#    at 419d153 and it is deliberately left untouched here.
#
# 7. A NAMED INSTALL-ONLY STEP. build.zig gains
#      const cli_step = b.step("cli", "Build and install the zbc CLI only");
#      cli_step.dependOn(&b.addInstallArtifact(exe, .{}).step);
#   so `zig build cli` compiles and installs the binary and nothing else. It
#   exists because the dogfood in item 6 analyzes this tree with this tree:
#   upstream puts `zbc src/` on the default step, so a plain `zig build` cannot
#   install the artifact while the analyzer has findings about its own source.
#   The dogfood is NOT weakened, shortened or removed: `b.default_step.dependOn
#   (&self_check.step)` is left exactly as upstream declares it, bare `zig build`
#   still runs it, and only an installer that names `cli` gets the install-only
#   path. `b.installArtifact(exe)` and the `run`/`test`/`fuzz` steps are
#   untouched. This is a build.zig hunk, so it does not move `source_digest`,
#   which is computed over src/**/*.zig only.
